# The Canal Gazette, its reports on the broken gate at Mill Lock and the duck race, and everyone
# quoted in them were made up for this lesson.
lesson reading-a-newspaper-report v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=reading levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A newspaper report"
  goal "Read a newspaper report for its news: who, what, where, when and why, whose words are whose, and whether the headline is fair."

  look {
    scene 36x16 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=end {
        passage p title="Lock gate breaks, boats wait two days" text="More than twenty narrowboats were stuck at Mill Lock on Tuesday and Wednesday after one of its wooden gates split, the canal trust said." width=17 numbers=false
        canallock l level=0.3 boat=1
      }
    }
    say """
      A newspaper report is built for a reader in a hurry. The headline gives the news in a few
      words. The opening paragraph says who, what, where, when and often why. After that come the
      details, the words of people who were there in speech marks, and last the background, which
      an editor can cut if the page is full. Everything outside the speech marks is the reporter's.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked reading.news-part k=0
    }
    show reading.news-part k=1 easy-k=2 hard-k=3
    show reading.news-five k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show reading.news-five k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show reading.news-who-said k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level medium hard {
      show reading.news-part k=2 hard-k=1
    }
  }
  story {
    show reading.news-number k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show reading.news-first k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show reading.news-first k=2 easy-k=2 hard-k=1
    show reading.news-number k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show reading.news-headline k=0 easy-k=1 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show reading.news-write k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  remember {
    say "The news comes first, the details next, the background last, and only words in speech marks are someone else's."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a news report has a shape made for its reader: headline, an opening that
    answers who, what, where and when, details and quotations, then background. The way in names a
    part of the report on the broken lock gate; the core answers a question word from the opening,
    says whose words a sentence is, records facts from the body (some needing two places in it),
    and decides which paragraph opens a report and which an editor would cut. The wrong idea to
    watch for is taking the reporter's own sentences as someone's quotation, and believing a
    headline that claims more than the facts. If the child is stuck, run a finger along the speech
    marks and say who is talking. Off the page, read the front page of a local paper together:
    find the five questions in its opening, and check the headline against the story. The Canal
    Gazette and its reports were made up for this lesson. England's programme of study for years 5
    and 6 asks children to "retrieve, record and present information from non-fiction".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a part is named once in the teacher's pen first, the questions ask where and when, the words to place are a witness's, the facts to record are in one sentence, the paragraph to put first is the duck race's, and the two-star's headlines include a vague one."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the part to name is the opening paragraph, the questions ask why and on which days, the words to place are the reporter's own, a fact needs times from two sentences (36 hours), and the two-star's wrong headlines are both claims the report does not support."
  }
}
